Now let me search for relevant human reviews in the dataset.Based on my reading of the paper and retrieval of relevant human reviews, I can now synthesize the weaknesses. The retrieved reviews most relevant to this benchmark-construction paper highlight issues around dataset exclusion criteria, circular evaluation metrics, and diversity claims that don't match the proposed methodology. Let me now write a focused weaknesses review.

---

## Weaknesses

**1. Circular definition of diversity.** The paper defines benchmark diversity entirely through reconstruction error — i.e., how well a source benchmark predicts model rankings on a target benchmark. This metric measures *coverage of other existing benchmarks*, not diversity in any absolute or application-meaningful sense. A benchmark that perfectly interpolates the ranking behavior of prior benchmarks could still be missing whole categories of real-world tabular datasets not represented in those benchmarks. The claim that TabPalooza has "superior diversity" is thus relative to the existing benchmark pool and does not constitute an independently validated diversity guarantee.

**2. Systematic exclusion biases the benchmark toward small-scale, low-dimensional, few-class data.** The dataset filtering removes all datasets with more than 50,000 training samples, more than 10,000 features, and (for classification) more than 10 classes. This removes a large and practically important segment of real-world tabular problems. Benchmarking conclusions drawn from TabPalooza may not generalize to large-scale, high-dimensional, or multi-class settings, yet the paper presents the benchmark as broadly representative for tabular machine learning.

**3. Stochastic dataset selection without stability analysis.** The final benchmark datasets are selected by randomly sampling one dataset per cluster from hierarchical clustering. No ablation study is provided to assess how sensitive the benchmark composition — and the resulting diversity and alignment metrics — is to different random seeds. Without such an analysis, it is unclear whether the reported diversity improvements are robust or artifacts of a fortunate random draw.

**4. Euclidean distance over raw meta-features is poorly justified.** The benchmark alignment pipeline computes pairwise dataset similarity using Euclidean distance in the 112-dimensional meta-feature space. These features have heterogeneous scales and semantic meanings (e.g., general statistics, information-theoretic quantities, landmarking performance), yet no normalization or feature weighting is applied. Euclidean distance in this space is unlikely to reflect meaningful dataset similarity and may systematically distort which datasets are considered nearest neighbors during alignment.

**5. Adaptation of classification-focused meta-features to regression is unvalidated.** PyMFE is explicitly designed for classification tasks. The paper adapts it to regression by discretizing the continuous target into 10 equal-frequency bins. This procedure introduces an arbitrary design choice (10 bins) and discards distributional information about the regression target. No ablation or analysis is presented to verify that the resulting meta-features meaningfully characterize regression datasets or that the bin count does not substantially alter the clustering and selection outcomes.

**6. Threshold and correlation method selection are chosen post-hoc on the same evaluation benchmarks.** The configuration for TabPalooza construction (Kendall+0.07 for classification, dCor+0.12 for regression) is selected by evaluating alignment against the same set of target benchmarks used to demonstrate superiority. This constitutes implicit overfitting to the held-out benchmarks, and it is unclear whether the selected configuration would generalize to new benchmarks or future tabular datasets not included in the current comparison pool.

**7. LLM-based Kaggle dataset validation lacks quality controls.** Kaggle dataset files are processed using DeepSeek-r1:32b and Qwen3:32b to identify target columns and task types. Only results consistent between the two models are retained. However, no human audit or external ground-truth verification is reported. There is no estimate of the error rate of this automated process, and the quality of the 335 classification and 251 regression Kaggle-sourced datasets — which constitute a substantial portion of the Universal Dataset Pool — is therefore uncertain.

**8. No downstream demonstration of practical utility.** The paper demonstrates that TabPalooza achieves lower reconstruction error relative to existing benchmarks but does not validate that conclusions drawn from TabPalooza (e.g., model rankings) are more predictive of real-world performance than those from existing benchmarks. An evaluation showing that model selection guided by TabPalooza rankings generalizes better to held-out real-world tasks would substantially strengthen the practical motivation for the benchmark.
