Now let me search for relevant human reviews in the dataset.Now let me read more of the paper and search for more relevant reviews.Based on my reading of the paper and the human reviews, I now have enough information to synthesize the weaknesses.Based on my reading of the paper and the relevant human reviews, here are the key weaknesses:

---

**Weaknesses**

**1. Evaluation metric circularity with training rewards.**
The direct reward finetuning explicitly optimizes CLIP-based and HPSv2 scores during training. Yet CLIP score is then used as a primary evaluation metric in Table 1, and HPSv2 closely corresponds to the Aesthetic Quality and Unified Reward scores reported there. This creates a circular evaluation: gains on these metrics may reflect reward optimization rather than genuine improvements in 3D generation quality. No held-out metrics that are independent of the reward signal—such as user-study ratings across a wider prompt set or geometry-based measures—are used to verify that improvements generalize beyond the optimized objectives.

**2. Text-to-pointmap generation is evaluated only qualitatively.**
The paper identifies text-to-pointmap generation as a key contribution, but evaluates it purely qualitatively, citing the absence of established benchmarks. Meanwhile, Table 5 evaluates pointmap reconstruction quality by feeding real input images into the stitched VAE—this validates the stitching component in isolation but says nothing about the quality of the full generative pipeline when starting from text. The absence of any quantitative end-to-end evaluation for this output modality leaves a significant portion of the claimed contribution unvalidated.

**3. Novel-view synthesis evaluation (Table 3) measures reconstruction, not generation.**
The NVS results report performance on the reconstruction task (real images fed into the stitched encoder), not on the text-to-3D generation task. This conflates the quality of the VAE component with the quality of the complete text-to-3D system. A user might interpret these improvements as reflecting generative quality, but they only capture how well the stitched decoder reproduces 3D representations from real-image latents.

**4. Modified DPG-Bench protocol complicates comparison.**
The paper deviates from the official DPG-Bench protocol by substituting the originally proposed language model with a more capable UnifiedReward LLM (Qwen 7B). While the authors frame this as an upgrade, this modification makes scores incomparable to any published DPG-Bench results and could disproportionately affect methods in unpredictable ways. No analysis is provided of how much this substitution changes scores relative to the original protocol.

**5. Small-scale human evaluation.**
The user study involves only 28 participants evaluating 14 randomly selected samples. Given that the benchmarks span object-centric (T3bench), scene-level (SceneBench), and long-prompt (DPG-bench) prompts with very different characteristics, drawing strong conclusions about method superiority from 14 samples is statistically fragile. The sample size is insufficient to demonstrate statistically significant preferences, especially given that individual generated samples can vary considerably in quality.

**6. No computational cost or inference time analysis.**
Direct reward finetuning requires backpropagating through the full denoising trajectory, which is substantially more expensive than standard supervised finetuning. The paper does not report training costs, GPU memory requirements, or inference time for VIST3A compared to baselines. Without this information, it is impossible to determine whether the quality improvements justify the computational overhead, or how VIST3A positions relative to methods on an efficiency-performance frontier.

**7. Limited analysis of failure modes and domain generalization.**
The fine-tuning uses DL3DV-10K and ScanNet, which are primarily real-world indoor and outdoor scene datasets. T3bench, one of the primary evaluation benchmarks, covers object-centric generation with single objects on clean backgrounds—a very different distribution. While the paper reports strong quantitative results, there is no analysis of failure cases, no discussion of what kinds of prompts the method handles poorly, and no study of how performance degrades outside training-distribution scene types.
